  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Golovinomyces eurybiarum is a fungal pathogen belonging to the powdery mildew family that affects a variety of plant hosts in New England. This obligate parasite forms characteristic white, powdery colonies on plant surfaces and can significantly impact plant health and aesthetics.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White Powdery Coating",
      "description": "Distinctive white, talc-like fungal coating on leaves, stems, and flowers that can be easily rubbed off",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Range",
      "description": "Infects multiple plant families in New England, making identification context-dependent on the affected plant species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Conidial Chains",
      "description": "Microscopic observation reveals barrel-shaped conidia arranged in chains, characteristic of the Golovinomyces genus",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Golovinomyces eurybiarum thrives in New England's cool, humid environments, particularly in areas with poor air circulation and moderate temperatures ranging from 60-75°F.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many plant pathogens, powdery mildew fungi don't require free water on leaf surfaces to infect plants—they can actually be inhibited by wet conditions, making dry, humid environments their ideal breeding ground.",
